    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 052355\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n455 PM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 00Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Major waterways such as the Salt and Gila Rivers will continue \n  to experience high volumes flows through at least the weekend \n  due to dam releases.\n\n- With the exception of a slight chance for mountain showers on \n  Friday, dry weather will be common through at least the middle \n  of next week. \n\n- Temperatures a few degrees below normal will warm near to just\n  above seasonal readings by this weekend.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Saturday/...\nAfternoon upper-air analysis reveals phasing shortwaves stretching \nfrom the Great Lakes down through the Southern Plains, while another \nweak disturbance transitions from the Pacific Northwest toward the \nNorthern Rockies. Even though it is located well off to our north, \nthe latter of these features will actually have some influence on \nconditions for parts our forecast area as we finish out the work \nweek. Before we get there though, prevailing quasi-zonal flow, which \nhas promoted multiple days of dry and tranquil conditions, will have \nto break down. The dissolving of the west-east flow will come in the \nform of the western vorticity lobe becoming heavily stretched out \nthanks to a developing anti-cyclone in its wake, forcing some of \nthis energy down across the Great Basin. Falling heights aloft \nassociated with this southern dive will eventually find their way \nover Arizona early Friday, deepening enough to become a cut-off low, \nbefore continuing on into northern Mexico. However, the expectation \ncontinues to be that this disturbance will be rather weak, resulting \nin minimal impacts. Synoptic and orographic ascent squeezing out \na few mountain showers and some breezy northerly winds over the \nlower Colorado River Valley thanks to a tight regional pressure \ngradient would likely be the most we see. Breezy conditions will \nlinger in Saturday morning, but areawide dry and tranquil \nconditions return soon thereafter as strong ridging takes over \nacross the Intermountain West. \n\nEven with the presence of the Friday cold-core, temperatures the \nnext few afternoons will be relatively steady, running the mid 70s \nto lower 80s for desert locations, with low 60s to low 70s being \ncommon for high terrain areas. Low and mid-level thermal profiles \nwill not see much fluctuation during this timeframe, owing to how \nshallow the upcoming system will be and how lower-troposheric \nheating will make up for any cooling. By Saturday, temperatures will \nrise to near and slightly above seasonal levels as the \naforementioned ridging migrates over the region. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Sunday through Wednesday/... \nSome variation of a Rex or omega block will define the southern \nstream flow pattern early next week as broad ridging dominates the\nnorthern Conus and northern stream jet energy retreats into \nsouthern Canada. While there are certainly differences among \nensemble members regarding the exact placement and intensity of \nquasi-cutoff features in the southern stream, this synoptic \npattern will promote surface pressure rises over the intermountain\nWest and dry N/NE boundary layer flow. As a result, periods of \nbreezy east winds are likely at times over the eastern CWA (very \ncommon occurrence this time of year), though speeds should not be \nparticularly strong or unusual. At some point early next week, \nmost ensemble members suggest the aforementioned cutoff feature \nopening and residual energy moving through the region, though it \nremains to be seen whether an introduction of mid/upper level \nmoisture can overcome the dry boundary layer airmass. The greatest\nodds and large preponderance of ensemble membership indicate dry \nweather persisting through the middle of the week. Forecast \nconfidence is very good that H5 heights will remain nearly \nconstant in a 580-584dm range resulting in a near persistence \ntemperature forecast with readings very near climatology. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2355Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nWinds will tend to follow their typical diurnal tendencies with\nspeeds aob 7 kt through the period. Extended periods of light and\nvariable winds are expected. Confidence is low in a westerly shift\nthis evening. Winds will likely just be light and variable, but\nmay tend to have a W/NW component, before reestablishing an\neasterly component overnight. FEW-SCT and at times BKN high clouds\nwill be common through the period. Some FEW clouds with decks aoa\n6-7 kt are expected to develop tomorrow afternoon/evening, mainly\nnorth and east of Phoenix. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected through the next\n24 hours under passing high clouds. Winds will be light through\nmid morning tomorrow with speeds aob 7 kt, periods of light and\nvariable to calm winds are also possible. By late tomorrow morning\nwind speeds will pick up and be around 10-15 kt, with some gusts\n(mainly at KBLH) up around 20 kt expected. Wind directions will be\nout of the west at KIPL prior to going NW late tomorrow morning.\nWinds will continue to exhibit a northerly component at KBLH. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAn isolated shower may affect mountains of eastern districts \nFriday, albeit with little chance of wetting rainfall while the \nremainder of the region remains dry through at least the middle of\nnext week. Minimum afternoon humidity levels will largely fall \ninto a 20-30% range in western districts and 30-50% in eastern \ndistricts. This will follow fair to good overnight recovery in \nwestern districts and good to excellent levels in eastern \ndistricts. 20-30 mph northerly gusts will be common in western \ndistricts Friday night through Saturday, while a period of \nenhances easterly winds will develop over eastern districts early \nnext week. Outside of these time frames, winds will be very light \nwith little to no gustiness. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...RW\nLONG TERM...18 \nAVIATION...Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
